#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492953} :activity/id #uuid "cb177df5-72c8-5d78-b659-9b78f111f63f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492225} {:answer/id #uuid "45356b43-9eb8-5668-952a-155b42bb6eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492353} {:answer/id #uuid "693adf90-92ac-557a-8ea3-07804f12f595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492427} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae7c7a-fba7-5249-b0f2-20cf7941b79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492461} {:answer/id #uuid "b321f881-cce5-5d65-bf3b-f929adf85f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492225} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea73b3dd-bf88-5f7b-9b97-f1ed24555540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e343068f-45c7-52dd-ac5c-c29d4a7a5cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143559" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by three machines, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ In a particular random sample, $31$ of the components were produced by machine $A,$ $34$ by machine $B,$ and $35$ by machine $C.$ On inspecting the sample for defective components, it was found that $2$ defective components were produced by machine $A,$ $1$  by machine $B,$ and $2$  by machine $C.$ Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective, we use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that there is a total of \n\n$$\n31+34+35=100\n$$\n\ncomponents in the sample.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $A$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $B$ $(1)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap C),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $C$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap C)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{100}+\\frac{1}{100}+\\frac{2}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48161813-f84d-504c-b8c4-db0136c2de37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9eae800-1856-54ad-a17f-88cd4e179f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089526} {:answer/id #uuid "b55beceb-c89e-57b6-9865-3a9a31ea6753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089527} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4d391-e2d8-5f7d-b816-23e4107fc373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089528} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb4624d-ab46-57be-a2fe-2083f5c47ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089529} {:answer/id #uuid "de8f08a7-ee40-5001-9780-39a8ddcc4bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186089530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e682799a-c8c0-5ebd-a2b3-68091a66eb2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d262f09b-86ba-5f7c-9ab7-53a1fc7fd15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143530" :question/problem "A math teacher splits her calculus class into three groups such that $35%$ of all the students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C. If $20%$ of the students in Group A obtained the top grade, $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$"} :step/id #uuid "64fd50f2-7184-51d1-b27f-040fb0e0ea7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b399609-7f79-5173-9ee0-8e611fd769c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492398} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492408} {:answer/id #uuid "837910a1-2e2b-5bf0-9265-dbc58e68c246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492483} {:answer/id #uuid "e355882d-ac3b-5bff-9a6a-e73a0f92e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.092" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492659} {:answer/id #uuid "ed962bd1-71d7-545c-8b5c-392d81210bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.062" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492408} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a0ca37-d77c-51fb-b507-a74b429aeacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa34c10c-c85d-5965-879f-040a2dcc2a58" :question/math-academy-id "q-143759" :question/problem "Ann has three email accounts. In the first account, she receives $40%$ of her emails, of which $10%$ are spam. In the second account, she receives $35%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. And in the third account, she receives $25%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the selected email is spam, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {A}_{1})+P(S\\cap {A}_{2})+P(S\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Ann receives $40%$ of her emails in the first account,  $35%$ in the second account, and $25%$ in the third account, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({A}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({A}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the emails received in the first account, $8%$ of the emails received in the first account, and $8%$ of the emails received in the third account are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|{A}_{1})=0.1,\\,P(S|{A}_{2})=0.08\\,P(S|{A}_{3})=0.08.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam is   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.1)(0.4)+(0.08)(0.35)+(0.08)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.04+0.028+0.02 \\\\  & =0.088.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "90883805-26ab-5fc5-bb58-296a7633b093" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24a9eb73-0014-5463-b63e-0a940d9e2582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492292} {:answer/id #uuid "5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492374} {:answer/id #uuid "5460928c-e102-597f-9db9-80c61e0a7e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492377} {:answer/id #uuid "7d996b1c-82f7-598f-be8d-1b498be533e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.049" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492469} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c243cf-613b-5eb8-9ed8-a7b18e1d6ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492374} :answer-field/id #uuid "03277a1a-6eea-5e2a-9857-810f8df632af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a98874b4-d2c1-50c9-83d1-a1b2a1981db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143721" :question/problem "Three factories produce the same tool for the market. Factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, of which $2%$ are defective. Factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tool is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.36.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the tools produced by factory $A$, $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $B$, and $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.34)+(0.05)(0.36) \\\\  & =0.006+0.017+0.018 \\\\  & =0.041.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2414de0-21e9-54d6-9573-da1fa9464bbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1443b175-b2e6-58e7-9266-46bb002bf1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492252} {:answer/id #uuid "731bab39-0cc7-53b5-8a98-4114c8993795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492446} {:answer/id #uuid "8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492493} {:answer/id #uuid "92f9cac9-b592-5ea1-b5f0-997e20fedd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492503} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe75c90-a8f8-5625-9747-315993606532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c481d0c-6bec-5a2b-817b-51131184fbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577" :question/math-academy-id "q-143978" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective, and box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly chosen from the box. If the probability that the selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ what is the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, and   \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is  from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since a box is chosen at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A$ and  $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$  we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(D|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective is $\\frac{1}{6}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a66ad3c1-21d4-525d-b81a-6c389b90b1cb"}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492215}]}
